# This image PREPARES THE REPOSITORY -- it is not the container the agent runs
# in. That is the OpenHands runtime, pinned separately in the benchmark runner
# (hybrid_gym_*/run_infer*.py). Here we clone at the pinned base commit, run the
# sanitizer, and re-initialise git history.
#
# It therefore needs exactly two things: git, and a python3 to run sanitize.py
# (stdlib only). No task in this project compiles, builds, installs, executes or
# modifies anything -- the model reads the code and explains it -- so no language
# toolchain is required, and ONE image correctly serves tasks in Go, Rust,
# Python, TypeScript, Java and JavaScript alike. That is a property of what the
# tasks do, not a convenience that happens to work.
#
# Previously golang:1.22-bookworm, which supplied git and python3 and never
# compiled a line of Go. It worked, but a Rust or Python bundle declaring a Go
# build environment misleads anyone reading the bundle. python:3.12-bookworm is
# language-neutral and carries both tools natively (verified in-image: git
# 2.39.5, Python 3.12.14). Sanitizer output was diffed between the old image's
# Python 3.11 and this 3.12 -- byte-identical, f-strings included, so PEP 701's
# tokenize changes do not affect it.
#
# Pinned by digest deliberately: a bundle's reproducibility rests on its pinned
# base commit AND its pinned image. A floating tag can be repointed upstream,
# which would silently change what a re-run of an old bundle is built from.
# To re-pin, resolve the tag's index digest and paste it here -- do not invent
# one:
#   docker pull python:3.12-bookworm
#   docker inspect --format='{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' python:3.12-bookworm
